BlitzMax.Format formats BlitzMax source text using the syntax model in
#BlitzMax.Language. It does not depend on the language server or an editor.

Create a #TBlitzMaxFormatOptions object to select formatting rules. A new
options object contains the defaults. Pass it to #TBlitzMaxFormatter.FormatText
to receive formatted text, or call #TBlitzMaxFormatter.Query to receive
#TBlitzMaxFormatEdits for an editor or another tool to apply.

## Format source text

Import the module, set only the options you want to change, and pass it a
source String. The formatter does not modify the original String.

```blitzmax
SuperStrict

Framework BRL.StandardIO
Import BlitzMax.Format

Local source:String = "SuperStrict~nIf True~nPrint ~qhello~q~nEnd If~n"
Local options:TBlitzMaxFormatOptions = New TBlitzMaxFormatOptions
options.thenStyle = "add"

Local formatted:String = TBlitzMaxFormatter.FormatText(source, "example.bmx", options)
Print formatted
```

This adds `Then` and indents the `Print` statement using the default tab style.
The formatted source is:

```blitzmax
SuperStrict
If True Then
	Print "hello"
End If
```

## Work with edits

An editor can request edits and apply them itself. Using the `source` and
`options` from the example above:

```blitzmax
Local edits:TBlitzMaxFormatEdits = TBlitzMaxFormatter.Query(source, "example.bmx", options)
For Local edit:TBlitzMaxFormatEdit = EachIn edits.items
	Print String(edit.start) + ".." + String(edit.finish) + ": " + edit.newText
Next

' Apply all edits in one step when a formatted String is needed.
Local formattedFromEdits:String = edits.Apply(source)
```

Edit start and finish positions are offsets into the original BlitzMax String.
Call #TBlitzMaxFormatEdits.Apply to apply all returned edits safely. Protocol
clients can convert these offsets to their own position encoding.

If the parser reports syntax errors, the formatter returns no edits and
FormatText returns the source unchanged. Multiline string contents and other
protected source regions retain their layout.
